.TH QREDSHIFT 1 "2026" "qredshift" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
qredshift \- Stateless Linux CLI for screen temperature, brightness, and gamma. Multi-monitor X11 and Wayland. Redshift alternative.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qredshift
.RB [ \-h ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-i ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR kelvin ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR brightness ]
.RB [ \-g
.IR gamma ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR target ]\ .\|.\|.
.RB [ \-xlib ]
.RB [ \-interp ]
.RB [ \-wd ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A lightweight, stateless command-line utility for Linux display color
management. QRedshift lets you adjust screen temperature (kelvin),
brightness, and gamma correction on individual monitors across X11
(XCB/Xlib) and Wayland (wlr-gamma-control-unstable-v1) display
servers.
.PP
It replaces redshift, sct, and gammastep with a C99 implementation
that compiles to a ~40KB binary.
.PP
Key features: native multi-monitor support, reverse gamma
reconstruction without stored state, Wayland daemon mode with
FIFO-based IPC, and a shared library plugin design that avoids
pulling libwayland-client as a dependency on X11 systems.
.PP
By default all connected displays are updated with the same values.
Per-display overrides are configured with the
.B \-d
flag.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h
Display usage help.
.TP
.B \-v
Show program version.
.TP
.B \-i
Show information about connected displays, including their current
estimated temperature, brightness, and gamma.
.TP
.BI \-t \ kelvin
Set temperature in Kelvin, from 1000 to 25000. Defaults to 6500 (no color
shift). Calling
.B qredshift \-t
with no value resets to the default.
.TP
.BI \-b \ brightness
Set brightness, from 0.1 to 1.0. Defaults to 1.0.
.TP
.BI \-g \ gamma
Set gamma, from 0.1 to 5.0. Defaults to 1.0.
.TP
.BI \-d \ target
Target a specific display by index or RandR output name, optionally
overriding temperature, brightness, or gamma for that display only. May be
repeated to target multiple displays in a single call. Any parameter not
specified in a
.B \-d
block falls back to the global value set by
.BR \-t / \-b / \-g .
.RS
.PP
Syntax:
.I id
.RB [ :t= K ]
.RB [ :b= B ]
.RB [ :g= G ]
.PP man
Examples:
.RS
.PP
.B qredshift \-t 4500 \-d 1
.RS 0
target display 1 by index, using the global temperature
.RE
.PP
.B qredshift \-t 4500 \-d HDMI\-1
.RS 0
target display by RandR output name
.RE
.PP
.B qredshift \-t 6500 \-d 1:t=4500:b=0.8
.RS 0
display 1 overrides temperature and brightness; other displays get the global 6500K
.RE
.PP
.B qredshift \-d 0:t=6500:b=1.0 \-d 1:t=4500:b=0.8
.RS 0
two displays, fully independent settings
.RE
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B \-xlib
Use the Xlib backend instead of the default XCB backend.
.TP
.B \-interp
Use the legacy interpolation method for Kelvin-to-RGB conversion.
.TP
.B \-wd
Start the Wayland daemon without applying initial values. Only
available on Wayland sessions. The daemon listens on a FIFO pipe
for subsequent commands.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success, non-zero on error (for example, no supported X11 or
Wayland display server detected, compositor missing required
protocols, or a display communication failure).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
Used with
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
to detect a Wayland session and locate the compositor socket.
If found and accessible, the Wayland backend is selected.
.TP
.B DISPLAY
Used to detect an X11 session. If
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
is not present or its socket is unreachable, the X11 backend is
selected.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xrandr (1),
.BR redshift (1),
.BR gammastep (1)
